WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Packing the Supreme Court
isn't unconstitutional.

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:06.106
Congress does have that power,

00:00:06.106 --> 00:00:09.376 align:start size:84% position:16%
but packing it with
this intention in mind

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:14.047 align:start size:97% position:3%
under this circumstance
really is anti-Constitutional.

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:17.017
It would undermine the
separation of power,

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:19.019
not as a bug, but as a feature.

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:20.954 align:start size:81% position:19%
And that's what
makes it so wrong.

00:00:20.954 --> 00:00:23.623
(calming music)

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:29.429 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin
and joining me today

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:32.799 line:0%
is the Senior Senator from
the great state of Utah,

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:34.434 line:0%
and the author of the new book,

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:38.738 align:start size:97% position:3%
Saving Nine: The Fight Against
the Left's Audacious Plan

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:42.475 align:start size:97% position:3%
to Pack the Supreme Court
and Destroy American Liberty.

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:45.779 align:start size:97% position:3%
Senator Mike Lee, finally
welcome to The Rubin Report.

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:46.746
- Thank you very much.

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:48.348
It's good to be with you.

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:50.817 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well, you have
handed me an awful lot,

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:52.819
that I'm talking
an awful lot about

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:54.954
in the title of the new book,

00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:57.590
and I wanna focus an
awful lot on that.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:01.194 align:start size:94% position:6%
But first, what is it like
to be on the short list

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:04.564
of what I would call sane people

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:06.900
in the United States
government right now?

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.036
- Yeah, it's sort of like being
one of the tallest buildings

00:01:10.036 --> 00:01:12.372
in Tooele, Utah, you might say.

00:01:12.372 --> 00:01:14.107
There aren't a lot of
tall buildings there.

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:15.708 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I take it short
buildings over there.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:18.144
Yeah, I mean, what is going on?

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:19.412
- Yeah so--

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:20.814
- What is going on right
now with the government,

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:23.283
and just the amount of
people that seemingly

00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:26.319 align:start size:88% position:13%
are doing nothing or
all of the wrong things?

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:30.056 line:0%
- Yeah, the problem is
less about doing nothing

00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:32.659 line:0%
and more about doing
all the wrong things.

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:35.428 align:start size:94% position:6%
When people start to think
that God is government,

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:36.696
and when they start to think

00:01:36.696 --> 00:01:38.364
that all government
needs to take place

00:01:38.364 --> 00:01:40.233
and transpire in Washington, DC,

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:42.268
that's a wicked combination.

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:45.071 align:start size:97% position:3%
A combination we've been
living under for many decades,

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:47.474 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's reached a
boiling point of late,

00:01:47.474 --> 00:01:50.110
and the left wants to
make it a lot worse.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:51.211
We're here to stop it.

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:55.582
- So that really is in many
ways what the book is about,

00:01:55.582 --> 00:01:57.517
and one of the things
that you talk about,

00:01:57.517 --> 00:01:59.786
and I've been trying to hit
on for a while on the show,

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:01.688
I have tried and I generally try

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:04.290
not to impune people's motives.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:05.792
But as you know,
the road to hell

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:07.360
is paved with good intentions.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.597
And it seems to
me at this point,

00:02:10.597 --> 00:02:12.232
what we are seeing
out of the left,

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:14.667 align:start size:84% position:16%
and virtually the
entire Democrat Party

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:18.538 align:start size:97% position:3%
is an intentional destruction
of so many of the things

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:22.509
that the founders had
built and had created

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:24.444
to allow us to all live free.

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:26.246
So before we get
into the specifics,

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:28.148
what do you think about
the intentions part?

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:30.583 align:start size:97% position:3%
Do you think a lot of what's
going on is intentional,

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:33.686 align:start size:97% position:3%
or do you think it's confusion
or a misunderstanding

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:36.923 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the documents or
something else altogether?

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:39.759
- You know, I tend to believe
that most people on the left

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:41.227 align:start size:84% position:16%
have at least
convinced themselves

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:43.763
that the place they want
to take us is a good place,

00:02:43.763 --> 00:02:45.398
that that would be
good for the country.

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:48.134
In the case of packing the
Supreme Court, for example,

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:50.603
I think they somehow believe

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:52.238
that that would take
us to a better place.

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:53.973
Now, I wrote Saving Nine

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:56.843
in order to explain why
the opposite is true.

00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:59.179 align:start size:88% position:13%
So, but regardless of
what their intention is,

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:02.048
I have no ability to jump
into someone else's mind,

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:03.216
to read their mind,

00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:06.252 align:start size:88% position:13%
to know the inner
thoughts and intentions

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:08.721 align:start size:88% position:13%
of their heart and their
soul and their mind.

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:13.726
But what I do know is certain
things that history tells us,

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:17.030
and history tells us that
there are some mistakes

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.998
we ought not want to repeat.

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:20.533
As Mitch McConnell likes to say,

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:23.636
"There's no education in the
second kick from the mule".

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:26.639
We've already been kicked by
the court packing mule once,

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:28.408
I described that at great length

00:03:28.408 --> 00:03:30.009
in chapter four of Saving Nine,

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:33.313 align:start size:94% position:6%
we do not want the
education that would come

00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:35.915
from the second kick
of that same mule.

00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:39.018 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So when you hear your
colleagues across the aisle

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:41.020
talking about packing the court,

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.522
and you're hearing
it all the time now,

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:46.159 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, it's virtually every
day someone is saying it,

00:03:47.293 --> 00:03:48.861 align:start size:88% position:13%
do you ever try to pull
them aside and say,

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:50.697
"Hey, do you guys realize

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:52.799 align:start size:84% position:16%
"that if you pack
the court this time,

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:55.268 align:start size:84% position:16%
"we'll just pack the
court next time?"

00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:58.571
Or that perhaps this
is a complete affront

00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:00.406
to the separation of powers,

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:02.442 align:start size:97% position:3%
or a series of other things
that you get into in the book,

00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:03.910
but you kind of say to them,

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.346 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Hey, do you realize what
you're gonna do here,

00:04:06.346 --> 00:04:08.982 align:start size:88% position:13%
"even if you temporarily
get what you want."

00:04:08.982 --> 00:04:10.383
- Of course, of course.

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:12.986
And you know, for
the time being,

00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:16.489 align:start size:84% position:16%
they're content with
talking about it,

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:19.025
but not really doing
it, at least not yet.

00:04:19.025 --> 00:04:20.526 align:start size:81% position:19%
I think they're
waiting to do that

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:24.464 align:start size:94% position:6%
perhaps until the Dobbs
ruling is actually issued.

00:04:24.464 --> 00:04:27.500
And maybe that's when they
start through it in earnest,

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.702
then it gets very, very serious.

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:31.671 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I think you're
right to point that out.

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:33.973
I don't think they
really want to go

00:04:33.973 --> 00:04:36.175
where they're trying
to take us either.

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:37.977
If they really think about it,

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:40.580 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I hope they will
read Saving Nine,

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.915 align:start size:94% position:6%
because regardless of
whether they agree with me

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:45.084
on any other policy issue,

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:46.319
they ought to be able to agree

00:04:46.319 --> 00:04:48.321
that the minute you
rip off that bandaid,

00:04:48.321 --> 00:04:50.423 align:start size:84% position:16%
and for reasons I
explain in the book,

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:55.228 align:start size:97% position:3%
the minute you start to
politicize the court formally,

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:58.264 align:start size:97% position:3%
as you would do by increasing
the number of Justices

00:04:58.264 --> 00:05:01.501 align:start size:94% position:6%
in order to achieve a
desired political outcome,

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:03.436
there is no stopping it.

00:05:03.436 --> 00:05:05.905
The Republicans, who
in modern history,

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:07.874
have never attempted
anything like this

00:05:09.042 --> 00:05:12.145
would be compelled
to do the same thing

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:13.946
the next time the shoe
is on the other foot.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:15.948
That is the next time the
House, and the Senate,

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:17.350
and the White House

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:18.718 align:start size:94% position:6%
are all under the control
of the same party.

00:05:18.718 --> 00:05:20.987
If the Democrats
increase by four,

00:05:20.987 --> 00:05:23.356 align:start size:97% position:3%
you can see Republicans
increasing it by four more.

00:05:23.356 --> 00:05:25.191
Maybe it'll be six next time.

00:05:26.392 --> 00:05:28.728
The result of that won't
be pleasant for anyone.

00:05:28.728 --> 00:05:32.298 align:start size:97% position:3%
The court, because of the
lifetime appointment guarantee

00:05:32.298 --> 00:05:34.534
of Article III of
the Constitution,

00:05:34.534 --> 00:05:36.436
this will be a one way ratchet,

00:05:36.436 --> 00:05:39.105
and the court will
expand a lot faster

00:05:39.105 --> 00:05:40.540
than it could ever shrink.

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:42.909 align:start size:94% position:6%
Before long it won't
look like a court at all,

00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:46.012
but like a political body,
and a large one at that.

00:05:46.012 --> 00:05:48.514
Eventually it'd look like
the intergalactic Senate

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:50.249
in the Star Wars movie series.

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:51.984
(Dave laughing)

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:54.687
- Believe it or not, I am
a big fan of the prequels.

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:57.290 align:start position:0%
And I think that they're looking
better and better over time

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:59.058
because of exactly
that, the you know,

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:02.061 align:start size:97% position:3%
how power just flips
depending on who's in charge.

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:05.565 align:start size:94% position:6%
At what point do you think
that packing the court

00:06:05.565 --> 00:06:09.769
is actually an assault on
the Constitution itself?

00:06:09.769 --> 00:06:12.438 align:start size:88% position:13%
In other words, that
one branch of government

00:06:12.438 --> 00:06:14.707
would in essence be destroying
another branch of government.

00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:16.376 align:start size:88% position:13%
They're supposed to have
checks and balances.

00:06:16.376 --> 00:06:17.777
But to me that's a,

00:06:17.777 --> 00:06:20.513
it's almost a violation
of the oath of office

00:06:20.513 --> 00:06:22.782
if your goal as a legislator

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:25.618 align:start size:81% position:19%
is to override
what the judiciary

00:06:25.618 --> 00:06:27.053
is supposed to be comprised of.

00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:28.221
- Yeah no, that's exactly right.

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:29.722
And I think if you're doing it,

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:30.923
the minute you're doing it

00:06:30.923 --> 00:06:33.760 align:start size:84% position:16%
not because the court
is short staffed,

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.128
I think in theory,

00:06:35.128 --> 00:06:37.196 align:start size:94% position:6%
that could be a legitimate
reason to do it.

00:06:37.196 --> 00:06:40.199
If at some unknown
point in the future,

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:43.403
there's widespread
consensus that there,

00:06:43.403 --> 00:06:45.671
the human resources on the court

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:49.008
have been outpaced
by the caseload,

00:06:49.008 --> 00:06:50.543
you could consider
an argument then.

00:06:50.543 --> 00:06:52.211
Even then you would
wanna be very careful

00:06:52.211 --> 00:06:55.448
what phasing in the increases.

00:06:55.448 --> 00:06:56.883
But whereas here,

00:06:56.883 --> 00:06:58.885 align:start size:94% position:6%
they're just talking
about a political outcome

00:06:58.885 --> 00:07:00.787 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they desire
they want to achieve,

00:07:00.787 --> 00:07:02.188
then this is an assault on that.

00:07:02.188 --> 00:07:04.190 align:start size:84% position:16%
Here's the interesting
thing about that.

00:07:05.324 --> 00:07:07.460 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's kind of an
irony built into it.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:10.029
Packing the Supreme Court
isn't unconstitutional.

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:13.299
Congress does have that power.

00:07:13.299 --> 00:07:16.569 align:start size:84% position:16%
But packing it with
this intention in mind

00:07:16.569 --> 00:07:21.240 align:start size:97% position:3%
under this circumstance
really is anti-Constitutional.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:24.110
It would undermine the
separation of power,

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.212
not as a bug, but as a feature,

00:07:26.212 --> 00:07:28.347 align:start size:81% position:19%
and that's what
makes it so wrong.

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:30.349
- But do you think
it matters to them?

00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:31.617
You know what I mean?

00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:33.519
Like they sort of, their
ends justify the means,

00:07:33.519 --> 00:07:34.987 align:start size:81% position:19%
so do you think
it really matters,

00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:36.756 align:start size:94% position:6%
meaning that they, if they
can go ahead and do it

00:07:36.756 --> 00:07:37.757
and get away with it,

00:07:37.757 --> 00:07:39.459
which seemingly you can get away

00:07:39.459 --> 00:07:41.561 align:start size:84% position:16%
with virtually
anything these days,

00:07:41.561 --> 00:07:43.329
then why not go ahead and do it?

00:07:43.329 --> 00:07:45.898 align:start position:0%
I know that's not your position,
but from their perspective.

00:07:45.898 --> 00:07:48.334 align:start size:97% position:3%
- From their perspective,
that often is a big motivator,

00:07:48.334 --> 00:07:51.304
and I think that is part
of why they're doing it.

00:07:51.304 --> 00:07:53.973 align:start size:84% position:16%
Not withstanding
some of these risks,

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:56.242 align:start size:88% position:13%
is that they're
looking at this saying,

00:07:56.242 --> 00:08:01.180
if we can do it and it would
please our base, why not do it?

00:08:02.582 --> 00:08:04.450 align:start size:97% position:3%
Why not at least try, maybe
we will swing and miss,

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:07.753
but we'll at least draw
a lot of great applause

00:08:07.753 --> 00:08:10.022
from our own base.

00:08:10.022 --> 00:08:11.424
But for the reasons I explain

00:08:11.424 --> 00:08:13.593
in chapter four of Saving Nine,

00:08:13.593 --> 00:08:15.928
even when you swing and miss,

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:17.964 align:start size:84% position:16%
even when you threaten
to pack the court,

00:08:17.964 --> 00:08:21.334
you propose court packing,
and the legislation fails,

00:08:21.334 --> 00:08:23.169
it can leave an indelible mark,

00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:25.738
a mark that we're
still paying for,

00:08:25.738 --> 00:08:30.743 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the, you know, lengthy
period of time that's elapsed

00:08:31.878 --> 00:08:33.646 align:start size:84% position:16%
since 1937 when
they tried it last.

00:08:33.646 --> 00:08:36.582
We're still suffering from
what happened to the court,

00:08:36.582 --> 00:08:40.987
what it did to the court
all that many years ago.

00:08:40.987 --> 00:08:44.724 align:start size:97% position:3%
85 years later, and we're
still paying for that mistake.

00:08:44.724 --> 00:08:47.159 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So you write about that
pretty extensively actually.

00:08:47.159 --> 00:08:48.828
So can you just go
into what happened

00:08:48.828 --> 00:08:50.596
those 85 years ago, yeah.

00:08:50.596 --> 00:08:53.399
- 85 years ago, you
had a Supreme Court

00:08:53.399 --> 00:08:57.470 align:start size:97% position:3%
that was doing its job and
was enforcing the outer limits

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:59.005
on Congress's authority,

00:08:59.005 --> 00:09:01.741
especially its authority
under the Commerce clause.

00:09:01.741 --> 00:09:03.242
The part that gives
Congress the power

00:09:03.242 --> 00:09:05.044
to regulate trade or commerce

00:09:05.044 --> 00:09:07.413
between the states and
the foreign nations.

00:09:07.413 --> 00:09:11.417
And FDR, President
Franklin D. Roosevelt

00:09:11.417 --> 00:09:13.019
was frustrated at the fact

00:09:13.019 --> 00:09:14.954 align:start size:84% position:16%
that the Supreme Court
was knocking down

00:09:14.954 --> 00:09:17.423
some of his signature
pieces of legislation,

00:09:17.423 --> 00:09:20.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
giving Congress and the
federal government as a whole

00:09:20.026 --> 00:09:23.129
unprecedented power
to deal with things

00:09:23.129 --> 00:09:25.631
in response to the
Great Depression.

00:09:25.631 --> 00:09:27.867
They wanted to be
able to regulate,

00:09:27.867 --> 00:09:31.837 line:0%
things like agriculture,
and mining, and labor,

00:09:31.837 --> 00:09:33.372 line:0%
and manufacturing,

00:09:33.372 --> 00:09:36.842 line:0%
even though historically
those had been regulated

00:09:36.842 --> 00:09:38.644
almost exclusively by the states

00:09:38.644 --> 00:09:40.980
and not the federal government.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:44.450
Now, the Supreme Court just
interpreting the Constitution

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:49.288
based on what it had always
meant was threatened by FDR.

00:09:49.288 --> 00:09:51.991
Just two years after
the court had moved

00:09:51.991 --> 00:09:55.494
into its new building, the
building it now occupies,

00:09:55.494 --> 00:09:57.363
on April 12th, 1937,

00:09:57.363 --> 00:10:00.499 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Supreme Court effectively
rewrote the Constitution.

00:10:00.499 --> 00:10:02.401
It rewrote the Commerce clause

00:10:02.401 --> 00:10:03.936
to give the federal
government the power

00:10:03.936 --> 00:10:07.440
to regulate anything that even
affected interstate commerce,

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:09.842 align:start size:88% position:13%
not just interstate
commercial transactions,

00:10:09.842 --> 00:10:12.511
or the corridors through
which they take place,

00:10:12.511 --> 00:10:15.047 align:start size:84% position:16%
but anything that
affects those things

00:10:15.047 --> 00:10:17.516 align:start size:84% position:16%
could now be
regulated by Congress.

00:10:17.516 --> 00:10:20.419 align:start position:0%
That meant labor, manufacturing,
agriculture, mining,

00:10:20.419 --> 00:10:21.721
all the things that FDR

00:10:21.721 --> 00:10:23.856 align:start size:88% position:13%
wanted to be able to
regulate comprehensively

00:10:23.856 --> 00:10:26.726
from Washington DC,
they could now do.

00:10:26.726 --> 00:10:30.763
This occurred as a result
of one vote switching.

00:10:30.763 --> 00:10:33.032
Associate Justice Owen Roberts,

00:10:33.032 --> 00:10:36.869
who had previously stood
by others on the court

00:10:36.869 --> 00:10:38.304 align:start size:84% position:16%
who had acknowledged
there were limits

00:10:38.304 --> 00:10:41.540 align:start size:94% position:6%
on the Congress's
Commerce clause authority,

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:42.842
he switched his vote.

00:10:42.842 --> 00:10:44.844
He joined with the
Liberals on the court

00:10:44.844 --> 00:10:47.013
and they rewrote
the Constitution.

00:10:47.013 --> 00:10:49.181
And it's a result of that,

00:10:49.181 --> 00:10:51.217 align:start size:97% position:3%
that we're now dealing
with the federal government

00:10:51.217 --> 00:10:53.486 align:start size:94% position:6%
that regulates every
aspect of human existence,

00:10:53.486 --> 00:10:56.856
that imposes $2 trillion of
regulatory compliance costs

00:10:56.856 --> 00:10:58.057
on the American economy,

00:10:58.057 --> 00:11:00.092 align:start size:88% position:13%
that requires Americans
to work for months

00:11:00.092 --> 00:11:01.193
out of every year,

00:11:01.193 --> 00:11:02.728
just to pay their federal taxes

00:11:02.728 --> 00:11:04.764
and then tells them that
even that's not enough

00:11:04.764 --> 00:11:07.733
'cause we're $31
trillion in debt.

00:11:07.733 --> 00:11:10.302 align:start size:94% position:6%
All of that, and the fact
that most of our laws

00:11:10.302 --> 00:11:11.704 align:start size:81% position:19%
at the federal
level are now made

00:11:11.704 --> 00:11:14.006 align:start size:94% position:6%
by unelected
unaccountable bureaucrats

00:11:14.006 --> 00:11:16.842
that can all be traced
to this switching time

00:11:16.842 --> 00:11:18.611
that saved nine,

00:11:18.611 --> 00:11:21.647
as I explain in chapter
four of Saving Nine.

00:11:21.647 --> 00:11:24.917 align:start size:94% position:6%
- So as a free market
guy, as a capitalist guy,

00:11:24.917 --> 00:11:26.619 align:start size:84% position:16%
as a guy that believes
in states' rights,

00:11:26.619 --> 00:11:28.621
and you want the free
exchange of goods,

00:11:30.056 --> 00:11:32.391
what should we be doing
to maybe get more people?

00:11:32.391 --> 00:11:34.326
Well, we can talk about
it and get more people

00:11:34.326 --> 00:11:36.062
to know about it and you
can write books about it,

00:11:36.062 --> 00:11:38.064
but what can we do
to get that case

00:11:38.064 --> 00:11:39.999
to be looked at again, perhaps?

00:11:39.999 --> 00:11:41.267
- Well yeah, so first of all,

00:11:41.267 --> 00:11:42.968 align:start size:88% position:13%
we need to get people
talking about federalism

00:11:42.968 --> 00:11:43.969
and separation of powers,

00:11:43.969 --> 00:11:45.471
because that's what we lost

00:11:45.471 --> 00:11:48.307 align:start size:97% position:3%
the last time that Democrats
tried to pack the court.

00:11:48.307 --> 00:11:49.942
When we get people
talking about that,

00:11:49.942 --> 00:11:53.813 align:start size:97% position:3%
we make it part of the
national political discourse.

00:11:53.813 --> 00:11:55.948 align:start size:81% position:19%
You shouldn't have
anyone running,

00:11:55.948 --> 00:11:57.550
especially as a Republican,

00:11:57.550 --> 00:12:00.753
but really from either
party for the US House,

00:12:00.753 --> 00:12:04.490 align:start position:0%
or the US Senate, or the
presidency of the United States,

00:12:04.490 --> 00:12:06.992
without them talking
about where they stand

00:12:06.992 --> 00:12:09.361
on how to restore federalism,

00:12:09.361 --> 00:12:11.497 align:start size:97% position:3%
the vertical protection
that keeps most of the power

00:12:11.497 --> 00:12:12.698
at the state local level,

00:12:12.698 --> 00:12:14.734
and how to restore
separation of powers,

00:12:14.734 --> 00:12:16.502 align:start size:84% position:16%
the horizontal
protection that says

00:12:16.502 --> 00:12:17.937
we're gonna have one
branch of government

00:12:17.937 --> 00:12:20.372 align:start size:97% position:3%
that makes the laws, Congress,
the legislative branch,

00:12:20.372 --> 00:12:23.042
one branch of government
headed by the President,

00:12:23.042 --> 00:12:25.277
the executive branch
that enforces the laws,

00:12:25.277 --> 00:12:27.313 align:start size:88% position:13%
and another branch that
interprets the laws

00:12:27.313 --> 00:12:29.315
headed by the Supreme Court.

00:12:29.315 --> 00:12:31.517
Those need to become mainstream

00:12:31.517 --> 00:12:34.253 align:start size:84% position:16%
political conversation
topics in America,

00:12:34.253 --> 00:12:36.622
again, especially
for federal races.

00:12:36.622 --> 00:12:38.958
That is really how we fix this.

00:12:38.958 --> 00:12:42.294 align:start size:94% position:6%
But in the meantime, we've
got to also counteract

00:12:42.294 --> 00:12:44.930
the clear and present
threat that we face

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:46.832
due to this now trending hashtag

00:12:46.832 --> 00:12:48.667
of hashtag expand the court,

00:12:48.667 --> 00:12:50.770 align:start size:84% position:16%
pioneered by Elizabeth
Warren and others,

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:52.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
who were trying overtly
to attack the independence

00:12:52.972 --> 00:12:55.040
of the federal judiciary.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:58.144 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So I know we could bash
the progressives and the left

00:12:58.144 --> 00:13:00.913 align:start position:0%
all day long, but it's obviously
not just them that have,

00:13:00.913 --> 00:13:03.482
you know, expanded the size of
government, and the budgets,

00:13:03.482 --> 00:13:04.717
and all of those things,

00:13:04.717 --> 00:13:06.685
and sending money to
all sorts of countries

00:13:06.685 --> 00:13:08.487
when we barely have a dime here.

00:13:08.487 --> 00:13:11.090 align:start size:97% position:3%
What do you think the
Republicans and Conservatives

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:12.591
could be doing better

00:13:12.591 --> 00:13:15.628
in terms of giving some of
the power back to the states

00:13:15.628 --> 00:13:17.429
and in terms of reigning
in some of the budgets

00:13:17.429 --> 00:13:18.664
and that kind of stuff?

00:13:18.664 --> 00:13:20.166 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah no, it's a
fair point you make.

00:13:20.166 --> 00:13:22.835
I mean, look, this expansion
of the size, scope, reach,

00:13:22.835 --> 00:13:24.570 align:start size:84% position:16%
and cost of the
federal government,

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:26.372
this has occurred
under the direction

00:13:26.372 --> 00:13:31.377
of Houses of Representatives,
of Senates and of White Houses

00:13:32.545 --> 00:13:34.914
of every conceivable
partisan combination.

00:13:34.914 --> 00:13:37.550
The problem is that we've
got the progressive mindset

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:40.286
that permeates the entirety
of the Democratic Party,

00:13:40.286 --> 00:13:44.290
and a significant portion
of the Republican Party.

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:48.027
And I think we have to focus
on that as Republicans and say,

00:13:48.027 --> 00:13:50.062 align:start size:84% position:16%
look, we're not the
party of progressives,

00:13:50.062 --> 00:13:53.199 align:start position:0%
we're the party of liberty,
we're the party of free markets,

00:13:53.199 --> 00:13:55.067
we're the party
of civil society.

00:13:55.067 --> 00:13:58.170
All those things atrophied
when we excessively flex

00:13:58.170 --> 00:13:59.505
the federal muscle,

00:13:59.505 --> 00:14:01.006 align:start size:81% position:19%
and that's what
we've got to undo.

00:14:01.006 --> 00:14:04.176
And in that respect, Saving
Nine is about much, much more

00:14:04.176 --> 00:14:07.446 align:start size:84% position:16%
than just preserving
the independence of
the Supreme Court

00:14:07.446 --> 00:14:09.248
and prohibiting court packing.

00:14:09.248 --> 00:14:12.284 align:start position:0%
It's about restoring federalism
and separation of powers.

00:14:12.284 --> 00:14:14.320
This book will teach
you how to do that.

00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:17.890 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So you mentioned politics
entering the court.

00:14:17.890 --> 00:14:19.725
Obviously at this point,

00:14:19.725 --> 00:14:22.328 align:start size:97% position:3%
because politics has become
our national religion,

00:14:22.328 --> 00:14:25.064 align:start size:81% position:19%
it seems almost
impossible not to.

00:14:25.064 --> 00:14:28.801
I had Judge Napolitano from
Fox on a couple weeks ago

00:14:28.801 --> 00:14:31.103 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the middle of the Ketanji
Brown Jackson hearing.

00:14:31.103 --> 00:14:34.740
And his position
was that basically

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:38.477 align:start size:94% position:6%
the Senate should confirm
a judge that is a,

00:14:38.477 --> 00:14:40.613
you know, nominated
by the President,

00:14:40.613 --> 00:14:43.549 align:start size:84% position:16%
barring something
really off the charts,

00:14:43.549 --> 00:14:46.385
like really, really
beyond unbelievable

00:14:46.385 --> 00:14:47.887
in the decision that they made,

00:14:47.887 --> 00:14:50.689 align:start position:0%
that basically the decision
should be left to the President,

00:14:50.689 --> 00:14:52.825 align:start size:84% position:16%
and short of something
really catastrophic

00:14:52.825 --> 00:14:54.326
the person should be confirmed.

00:14:54.326 --> 00:14:56.962
Now we watch confirmation
hearings and on both sides,

00:14:56.962 --> 00:14:58.564
it's all sorts of, you know,

00:14:58.564 --> 00:15:00.866 align:start size:84% position:16%
dredging out all kinds
of horrible things

00:15:00.866 --> 00:15:04.036
or made up things in the
case of Brett Kavanaugh,

00:15:04.036 --> 00:15:05.704
perhaps in Ketanji Brown's case,

00:15:05.704 --> 00:15:07.806
the fact that she couldn't
tell you what a woman was,

00:15:07.806 --> 00:15:11.677
maybe that was a catastrophic
thing that would've qualified.

00:15:11.677 --> 00:15:13.178
But what's your general position

00:15:13.178 --> 00:15:15.514
on when a President
nominates someone,

00:15:15.514 --> 00:15:17.983 align:start size:88% position:13%
what the process should
actually look like?

00:15:17.983 --> 00:15:21.887 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Look, I make no
mystery about the fact

00:15:21.887 --> 00:15:26.225 align:start position:0%
that when there is a
Democratic President in office,

00:15:26.225 --> 00:15:29.161
I understand that we're not
gonna get the same nominees

00:15:29.161 --> 00:15:32.364 align:start size:84% position:16%
as we would when
there's a Republican

00:15:32.364 --> 00:15:34.800
holding the office
of the presidency.

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:37.503
Still, it still matters.

00:15:37.503 --> 00:15:39.371 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it still matters
to me to review

00:15:39.371 --> 00:15:41.473
each candidate individually

00:15:41.473 --> 00:15:45.978
and figure out whether he or
she understands, respects,

00:15:45.978 --> 00:15:48.847 align:start size:84% position:16%
appreciates, and will
defiantly protect

00:15:48.847 --> 00:15:50.683
the independence
of the judiciary,

00:15:50.683 --> 00:15:55.688
and will understand that
each case is to be decided

00:15:56.822 --> 00:15:59.491
on the basis of its own merits

00:15:59.491 --> 00:16:02.661
and on the basis of
what the law says,

00:16:02.661 --> 00:16:04.697
on the basis of the law,

00:16:04.697 --> 00:16:07.900 align:start size:94% position:6%
as it was understood at
the time of its enactment,

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:10.569
if a statute or at the
time of its ratification,

00:16:10.569 --> 00:16:13.305
if it's a provision
of the Constitution.

00:16:13.305 --> 00:16:15.307
And so I'll vote
yes for a nominee,

00:16:15.307 --> 00:16:18.310
even if that nominee doesn't
share my political worldview,

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:22.481
if I think that nominee
is sufficiently committed

00:16:22.481 --> 00:16:23.682
to the judicial task.

00:16:23.682 --> 00:16:25.184
If not, I won't.

00:16:25.184 --> 00:16:28.854
So I don't know that
that ought to ever mean

00:16:28.854 --> 00:16:30.723
blind deference
to any President,

00:16:30.723 --> 00:16:32.257
whether that President

00:16:32.257 --> 00:16:35.194 align:start size:88% position:13%
is of your own
political party or not.

00:16:35.194 --> 00:16:37.029 align:start size:84% position:16%
It certainly doesn't
mean that to me,

00:16:37.029 --> 00:16:39.965 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I don't think it should
to anyone else either.

00:16:39.965 --> 00:16:41.734
- You mentioned the Dobbs
case before, you know,

00:16:41.734 --> 00:16:44.903
it's kind of bizarre, you
know, this leak happens,

00:16:44.903 --> 00:16:47.406 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, now it's probably
six weeks ago or so,

00:16:47.406 --> 00:16:51.310
and basically not only are we
not talking about it anymore,

00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:52.511 align:start size:81% position:19%
but it doesn't
seem like anyone's

00:16:52.511 --> 00:16:55.014
trying to track down the leaker.

00:16:55.014 --> 00:16:56.682 align:start size:88% position:13%
Are you aware of
anything that's going on

00:16:56.682 --> 00:16:59.184 align:start position:0%
that maybe we're not fully
aware of through the media yet?

00:16:59.184 --> 00:17:02.654
And what do you think
that, the leak itself,

00:17:02.654 --> 00:17:05.057 align:start size:88% position:13%
has done to the
integrity of the court?

00:17:05.057 --> 00:17:06.692
- You know, until
fairly recently,

00:17:06.692 --> 00:17:09.962
I was convinced that they
had identified the leaker,

00:17:09.962 --> 00:17:12.164
and they just had made
a strategic decision

00:17:12.164 --> 00:17:15.267 align:start size:97% position:3%
within the court not to
announce the leaker's identity

00:17:15.267 --> 00:17:17.603 align:start size:84% position:16%
until after the Dobbs
case was decided,

00:17:17.603 --> 00:17:21.240
which may not happen until
the end of the court's term,

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:24.143
which usually wraps
up in the final days

00:17:24.143 --> 00:17:25.911
of the month of June.

00:17:25.911 --> 00:17:27.579
Now but then a few days ago,

00:17:27.579 --> 00:17:29.915
news reports started to surface

00:17:29.915 --> 00:17:32.317 align:start position:0%
suggesting that the
investigation is still ongoing,

00:17:32.317 --> 00:17:34.053
that they've asked law clerks,

00:17:34.053 --> 00:17:36.422
there are four for each Justice,

00:17:36.422 --> 00:17:40.726
to submit their phone records
and submit sworn affidavits

00:17:40.726 --> 00:17:44.196
about their conduct
of this in this case

00:17:44.196 --> 00:17:45.964
about what, if anything,

00:17:45.964 --> 00:17:49.968
they have done with copies
of Supreme Court opinions.

00:17:49.968 --> 00:17:53.439
So it seems to be that the
investigation is ongoing.

00:17:53.439 --> 00:17:55.674 align:start size:88% position:13%
I do think they'll
figure it out who it is,

00:17:55.674 --> 00:17:58.477 align:start size:97% position:3%
'cause we're dealing with a
fairly finite group of people.

00:17:58.477 --> 00:18:01.013
And it's not that
hard to figure out,

00:18:01.013 --> 00:18:02.481
I think we'll find out.

00:18:02.481 --> 00:18:05.751 align:start size:94% position:6%
Whoever it is, if as I
suspect it is a law clerk,

00:18:05.751 --> 00:18:07.586
they should never
practice law again.

00:18:07.586 --> 00:18:09.421 align:start size:84% position:16%
They should be
disbarred for this.

00:18:09.421 --> 00:18:11.990
Because this is handling
sensitive information,

00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:14.359
information that was
not theirs to release.

00:18:14.359 --> 00:18:16.995 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you know, as a law clerk,
they drill this into you.

00:18:16.995 --> 00:18:19.465
It was made clear to me
when I was a law clerk

00:18:19.465 --> 00:18:21.600
at the Supreme Court
for Justice Alito,

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:26.605 align:start size:97% position:3%
you don't take draft opinions
out of the building ever.

00:18:28.006 --> 00:18:29.274 align:start size:97% position:3%
You don't take it to lunch,
you don't take it home.

00:18:29.274 --> 00:18:30.209
It shouldn't even
leave your office

00:18:30.209 --> 00:18:31.477
when you're finished with it.

00:18:31.477 --> 00:18:33.378
You can't just throw
it in the waste basket,

00:18:33.378 --> 00:18:36.715
you've got to put it
in this special bag,

00:18:36.715 --> 00:18:39.785 align:start size:84% position:16%
a bag whose contents
are retrieved at the
end of every day,

00:18:39.785 --> 00:18:43.655
they retrieve the contents,
they shred the documents,

00:18:43.655 --> 00:18:46.725
not just once but twice,
turning it into a confetti.

00:18:46.725 --> 00:18:48.861
And then they put it
in an incinerator,

00:18:48.861 --> 00:18:51.296
and burn it into
this black powder.

00:18:51.296 --> 00:18:52.631 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then they add a
little bit of water to it,

00:18:52.631 --> 00:18:56.168
and it creates this tar
like slurry structure

00:18:56.168 --> 00:18:58.604 align:start size:81% position:19%
before it ever
leaves the custody

00:18:58.604 --> 00:19:00.172
of the Supreme Court
of the United States.

00:19:00.172 --> 00:19:01.707
That's how seriously they take

00:19:01.707 --> 00:19:04.610
the confidentiality of the
opinion drafting process.

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:07.513 align:start size:84% position:16%
So whoever did this
knows darn well

00:19:07.513 --> 00:19:10.115 align:start size:88% position:13%
what those procedures
are and why they matter.

00:19:10.115 --> 00:19:12.084
That's why that person
should never be permitted

00:19:12.084 --> 00:19:13.452
to practice law again.

00:19:13.452 --> 00:19:15.721 align:start size:81% position:19%
I do think they'll
figure it out.

00:19:15.721 --> 00:19:18.056
- So assuming they do figure
it out, or even if they don't,

00:19:18.056 --> 00:19:20.559 align:start size:84% position:16%
what do you think it's
done just generally

00:19:20.559 --> 00:19:22.427
to the integrity of the court,

00:19:22.427 --> 00:19:25.664 align:start size:94% position:6%
the ability for the
court to make a decision,

00:19:25.664 --> 00:19:27.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
hold a decision until
the time is right

00:19:27.833 --> 00:19:29.268
for it to be released,

00:19:29.268 --> 00:19:31.770 align:start size:94% position:6%
and just the way the
media reacts to all of it,

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:34.339
because obviously this
was leaked to Politico,

00:19:34.339 --> 00:19:37.209
they had their decision on
whether to release it or not,

00:19:37.209 --> 00:19:39.011
or verify it in other ways,

00:19:39.011 --> 00:19:40.946 align:start size:88% position:13%
from everything we
understand it is legit,

00:19:40.946 --> 00:19:43.048
but just the general
standing of the court,

00:19:43.048 --> 00:19:44.516
which to me,

00:19:44.516 --> 00:19:45.884
the left is constantly
trying to subvert anyway,

00:19:45.884 --> 00:19:47.352
not just through packing,

00:19:47.352 --> 00:19:50.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
but generally just assault
all of the decisions.

00:19:50.389 --> 00:19:52.491
- Yeah, I think
it's devastating.

00:19:52.491 --> 00:19:53.759
I think it's devastating.

00:19:53.759 --> 00:19:56.762
Like the court has
enjoyed a history

00:19:56.762 --> 00:19:59.264
in which this has not occurred.

00:19:59.264 --> 00:20:02.367
Now the left wing media is
fond of saying things like,

00:20:02.367 --> 00:20:03.969
"Oh, this isn't the first
time this has happened.

00:20:03.969 --> 00:20:04.970
"Other leaks have happened."

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:07.005
Yeah sure, every
once in a while,

00:20:07.005 --> 00:20:10.108
maybe once or twice in a
decade word will leak out

00:20:10.108 --> 00:20:13.312 align:start size:97% position:3%
maybe an hour or two before
an opinion is released

00:20:13.312 --> 00:20:15.881
suggesting that the person knows

00:20:15.881 --> 00:20:17.349
what the answer is gonna be.

00:20:17.349 --> 00:20:20.052 align:start size:94% position:6%
And most of the time it's
a 50-50 guess anyway,

00:20:20.052 --> 00:20:22.955
it's like betting on red or
black at the roulette wheel.

00:20:22.955 --> 00:20:25.657
You're gonna be right on
average half the time.

00:20:25.657 --> 00:20:27.659 align:start size:81% position:19%
And sometimes they
get that right.

00:20:27.659 --> 00:20:31.363
But they've never leaked
an entire draft opinion.

00:20:31.363 --> 00:20:33.465
This has never happened before.

00:20:33.465 --> 00:20:37.269
There's been a network of
trust within the court,

00:20:37.269 --> 00:20:41.640
and there's been unanimous
support within the court

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:44.876 align:start position:0%
and its support staff in making
sure this doesn't happen.

00:20:44.876 --> 00:20:48.080 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think we can stem
the damage moving forward,

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:51.783
as long as the court takes
additional security steps

00:20:51.783 --> 00:20:53.252 align:start size:84% position:16%
to make sure this
never happens again.

00:20:53.252 --> 00:20:55.454 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I think they're gonna
have to put in place

00:20:55.454 --> 00:20:57.990
some sophisticated watermarks

00:20:57.990 --> 00:20:59.925
behind every draft
opinion from now on

00:20:59.925 --> 00:21:02.728 align:start size:81% position:19%
that will identify
that the person

00:21:02.728 --> 00:21:04.496 line:0%
who had access to that watermark

00:21:04.496 --> 00:21:06.331 line:0%
so that a leak like
this can't happen.

00:21:06.331 --> 00:21:10.202 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
And if it does happen,
the person will be
immediately found.

00:21:10.202 --> 00:21:12.337
- So you mentioned
federalism earlier

00:21:12.337 --> 00:21:14.740
and states' rights, obviously,

00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:16.808 align:start size:88% position:13%
as I mentioned to you
right before we started,

00:21:16.808 --> 00:21:19.645 align:start size:97% position:3%
I spent the last eight years
of my life in California.

00:21:19.645 --> 00:21:21.913 align:start size:84% position:16%
I now live in the
free state of Florida.

00:21:21.913 --> 00:21:23.382
And as I've been
saying on the show,

00:21:23.382 --> 00:21:26.418 align:start size:97% position:3%
I genuinely feel like I
live in a different country,

00:21:26.418 --> 00:21:29.655
not in a different state,
but in a different country.

00:21:29.655 --> 00:21:32.891 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm wondering what do you
see differently on the ground

00:21:32.891 --> 00:21:36.028 align:start size:94% position:6%
in terms of what your
constituents want in Utah,

00:21:36.028 --> 00:21:39.097
and the way that you feel
that you can govern there,

00:21:39.097 --> 00:21:41.633 align:start size:84% position:16%
versus what goes on
when you show up in DC

00:21:41.633 --> 00:21:42.834
and what you have to deal with?

00:21:42.834 --> 00:21:44.136
- Yeah, that's a good question.

00:21:44.136 --> 00:21:48.507
Look, most Americans,
it's true of Utahans,

00:21:48.507 --> 00:21:51.076 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I'm pretty sure it's
true of Californians

00:21:51.076 --> 00:21:53.879 align:start size:94% position:6%
and people in
Massachusetts, in Wyoming,

00:21:53.879 --> 00:21:55.480
or whatever state
you wanna identify,

00:21:55.480 --> 00:21:58.383 align:start size:84% position:16%
most Americans
intuitively resonate

00:21:58.383 --> 00:22:00.052
with the concept of federalism.

00:22:01.186 --> 00:22:03.121 align:start size:88% position:13%
And by the way, I refer
to it by that title

00:22:03.121 --> 00:22:04.589
rather than as states' rights,

00:22:04.589 --> 00:22:08.093 align:start size:97% position:3%
'cause my belief is that a
state is a unit of government.

00:22:08.093 --> 00:22:11.596
Units of government don't have
rights, they have authority.

00:22:11.596 --> 00:22:14.666
Authority is almost the
opposite of a right.

00:22:14.666 --> 00:22:18.403 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it's recognizing
the appropriate sphere

00:22:18.403 --> 00:22:20.072
of government authority.

00:22:20.072 --> 00:22:21.573
That's what federalism is.

00:22:21.573 --> 00:22:24.009
And most people understand
that federalism makes sense

00:22:24.009 --> 00:22:26.111
because it allows
for more Americans

00:22:26.111 --> 00:22:29.081
to gain access to more of the
kind of government they want,

00:22:29.081 --> 00:22:31.717 align:start size:97% position:3%
and less of the kind of
government they don't want.

00:22:31.717 --> 00:22:34.152
So for instance, in Utah,
it's pretty well understood

00:22:34.152 --> 00:22:35.721
that we'd rather not have

00:22:35.721 --> 00:22:39.725
a government-funded single
payer healthcare system.

00:22:39.725 --> 00:22:42.327
We have enough experiences

00:22:42.327 --> 00:22:44.296
going to the post
office or the DMV

00:22:44.296 --> 00:22:48.367
to know that we don't want
government being our doctor.

00:22:48.367 --> 00:22:50.869
People in Vermont I'm told

00:22:50.869 --> 00:22:54.005
would much prefer a single
payer government-funded,

00:22:54.005 --> 00:22:55.874 align:start size:81% position:19%
government-run
healthcare system.

00:22:55.874 --> 00:22:58.510 align:start size:94% position:6%
If we respected federalism
more completely,

00:22:59.978 --> 00:23:03.448 align:start size:97% position:3%
then more states would be able
to do exactly as they want.

00:23:03.448 --> 00:23:05.784 align:start size:88% position:13%
Vermont would be able
to go in that direction,

00:23:05.784 --> 00:23:08.019
Utahans wouldn't do that.

00:23:08.019 --> 00:23:11.356
That's what federalism is
supposed to be all about.

00:23:11.356 --> 00:23:12.991
That's why it's protected

00:23:12.991 --> 00:23:15.694
in the text of the original
Constitution doubled down on

00:23:15.694 --> 00:23:18.029
in the 10th Amendment,
but we've neglected it.

00:23:18.029 --> 00:23:20.198 align:start size:84% position:16%
Federalism still
has great potential.

00:23:20.198 --> 00:23:22.501 align:start size:84% position:16%
We just have to
allow it to be used.

00:23:22.501 --> 00:23:24.703 align:start size:88% position:13%
And for that to happen,
people in Congress

00:23:24.703 --> 00:23:26.371
have got to stop pretending

00:23:26.371 --> 00:23:29.574 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we are a general
purpose legislative body.

00:23:29.574 --> 00:23:32.077 align:start size:97% position:3%
We're supposed to be in charge
of just a few basic things.

00:23:32.077 --> 00:23:34.746
National defense, declaring
war, weights and measures,

00:23:34.746 --> 00:23:37.349 align:start size:88% position:13%
trademarks, copyrights,
and patents,

00:23:37.349 --> 00:23:39.918 align:start size:97% position:3%
bankruptcy laws, regulating
trade or commerce

00:23:39.918 --> 00:23:41.420
between the states
with foreign nations

00:23:41.420 --> 00:23:43.889 align:start size:97% position:3%
and with the Indian tribes,
and that's about it.

00:23:43.889 --> 00:23:47.092 align:start position:0%
There are a few others, but
there's not much more than that.

00:23:47.092 --> 00:23:49.227 align:start size:88% position:13%
Everything else is
supposed to be reserved

00:23:49.227 --> 00:23:51.096
for the states and localities.

00:23:51.096 --> 00:23:52.964 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You know, it's funny because
I asked you this already,

00:23:52.964 --> 00:23:54.533
but when I hear you say that,

00:23:54.533 --> 00:23:56.701
and lay it out so cleanly
and clearly and obviously,

00:23:56.701 --> 00:23:59.604 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's just like what the
bell in my head goes off of,

00:23:59.604 --> 00:24:02.374 align:start position:0%
I just don't think any Democrat
Senators care about that.

00:24:02.374 --> 00:24:04.443 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think a certain amount
of Republicans don't,

00:24:04.443 --> 00:24:06.878 align:start size:88% position:13%
but I honestly don't
think that any Democrat,

00:24:06.878 --> 00:24:08.246
I cannot name one.

00:24:08.246 --> 00:24:10.348
If you can name one,
I'd love to hear it,

00:24:10.348 --> 00:24:13.685 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I just don't think
they care about what it is,

00:24:13.685 --> 00:24:15.654
what you guys are
supposed to do.

00:24:15.654 --> 00:24:17.589
- Yeah, I think that's right.

00:24:17.589 --> 00:24:20.292
For two principle
reasons I don't.

00:24:21.493 --> 00:24:26.364
Number one, if they can
win, they can win bigly,

00:24:27.499 --> 00:24:29.835
as they might be
inclined to put it.

00:24:29.835 --> 00:24:32.871
Number two, they are winning

00:24:32.871 --> 00:24:35.240 align:start size:88% position:13%
in that they have
federalized everything.

00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:38.009 align:start size:88% position:13%
And this is the ultimate
progressive dream

00:24:38.009 --> 00:24:42.147 align:start size:97% position:3%
is to consolidate as much
government power as possible,

00:24:42.147 --> 00:24:44.716
consolidate it in the
national government.

00:24:44.716 --> 00:24:47.853 align:start size:94% position:6%
That way states won't
even have the opportunity

00:24:47.853 --> 00:24:52.157
of experimenting with a lower
profile system of government.

00:24:52.157 --> 00:24:53.592
Even though they themselves

00:24:53.592 --> 00:24:56.561
might like certain
aspects of federalism

00:24:56.561 --> 00:24:58.463
in these leftist states,

00:24:58.463 --> 00:25:02.367
they can't tolerate the
thought of other states,

00:25:02.367 --> 00:25:05.237
states like mine, states
like the state of Utah,

00:25:05.237 --> 00:25:06.705
doing it differently.

00:25:06.705 --> 00:25:08.306
They don't want the competition,

00:25:08.306 --> 00:25:11.076
and they also think that
in order to redeem us,

00:25:11.076 --> 00:25:12.844
in order to save
us from ourselves,

00:25:12.844 --> 00:25:15.146
we've got to nationalized
all of our law making.

00:25:15.146 --> 00:25:16.681
What does that get us?

00:25:16.681 --> 00:25:19.351
Well it gets us a whole lot of
contention, it gets us riots,

00:25:19.351 --> 00:25:24.022 align:start size:88% position:13%
it gets, it causes us
to create something akin

00:25:24.022 --> 00:25:26.124
to an Imperial presidency

00:25:26.124 --> 00:25:29.261
in which that people seem to
worship government increasingly

00:25:29.261 --> 00:25:33.098
thinking that the bigger,
the government in question,

00:25:33.098 --> 00:25:34.699
the better off we all are,

00:25:34.699 --> 00:25:37.369 align:start size:84% position:16%
reinforcing the
progressive mindset.

00:25:37.369 --> 00:25:39.471
- I thought you were gonna make
a Palpatine reference there.

00:25:39.471 --> 00:25:40.906
I felt it coming.

00:25:40.906 --> 00:25:43.041 align:start position:0%
- It could have come, but yeah,
it couldn't quite make it.

00:25:44.442 --> 00:25:48.213
- So on something like
the 40 billion to Ukraine

00:25:48.213 --> 00:25:50.382
that the Senate just voted on,

00:25:50.382 --> 00:25:53.184 align:start size:97% position:3%
obviously your counterpart
there in Utah did vote for it,

00:25:53.184 --> 00:25:54.920
Mitt Romney did vote for it.

00:25:54.920 --> 00:25:57.989
You know, from my audience's
perspective, for the most part,

00:25:57.989 --> 00:26:00.058
it's like, man, we
have so many things

00:26:00.058 --> 00:26:01.493
we could be fixing here

00:26:01.493 --> 00:26:03.562 align:start size:94% position:6%
if we even have this
money in the first place,

00:26:03.562 --> 00:26:04.796
which we kind of don't have,

00:26:04.796 --> 00:26:06.932
but we seemingly
always have money

00:26:06.932 --> 00:26:09.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
for other people's
problems, not for our own.

00:26:09.768 --> 00:26:12.938 align:start size:97% position:3%
How do you kind of deal with
that internal battle of,

00:26:12.938 --> 00:26:14.606
again, what you're
dealing with in DC,

00:26:14.606 --> 00:26:16.608
and what kind of
the machine wants,

00:26:16.608 --> 00:26:18.376
versus what your
own people want,

00:26:18.376 --> 00:26:20.845
and for a guy like you, you
actually care about budgets,

00:26:20.845 --> 00:26:22.581
and I have a suspicion you know

00:26:22.581 --> 00:26:24.583
that the 40 billion
doesn't really exist.

00:26:24.583 --> 00:26:25.984
Is that fair to say?

00:26:25.984 --> 00:26:28.820
- Yeah, so I looked
at that legislation.

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:30.789 align:start size:84% position:16%
I went through it with
a fine tooth comb,

00:26:30.789 --> 00:26:32.958 align:start size:94% position:6%
and what I found were
some significant problems.

00:26:32.958 --> 00:26:36.127
Number one, we're dealing
with rampant inflation

00:26:36.127 --> 00:26:37.596
in this country.

00:26:37.596 --> 00:26:40.498
The people in Utah are
paying an additional $751

00:26:40.498 --> 00:26:43.802
each month every month for
their basic living expenses,

00:26:43.802 --> 00:26:45.337
relative to what
they were paying

00:26:45.337 --> 00:26:47.639
at the beginning
of just last year.

00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:52.210 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is a huge
defacto tax increase

00:26:52.210 --> 00:26:54.579
that disproportionately harms

00:26:54.579 --> 00:26:56.247
the poor and middle
class of my state.

00:26:56.247 --> 00:26:58.617
And it's entirely foreseeable,

00:26:58.617 --> 00:27:03.321
it is the natural consequence
of excessive federal spending.

00:27:03.321 --> 00:27:06.157 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so I thought
that the Ukraine bill

00:27:06.157 --> 00:27:08.193
should have been paid for,
should have been offset,

00:27:08.193 --> 00:27:10.495 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I introduced an
amendment to that effect

00:27:10.495 --> 00:27:11.696
that would've done that.

00:27:11.696 --> 00:27:14.499 align:start size:84% position:16%
Also in that same
amendment was language

00:27:14.499 --> 00:27:17.702
that would've stripped
out Title V of that bill.

00:27:17.702 --> 00:27:21.206 align:start position:0%
Title V is where the worst
spending abuses within the bill

00:27:21.206 --> 00:27:22.407
could be found,

00:27:22.407 --> 00:27:25.543 align:start size:84% position:16%
or we're talking a
large amount of money,

00:27:25.543 --> 00:27:27.879
about $15 billion or so

00:27:27.879 --> 00:27:31.950 align:start position:0%
that would've gone to activities
in the state department

00:27:31.950 --> 00:27:35.920 align:start size:97% position:3%
that are at best, tangentially
related to Ukraine.

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:37.889 align:start size:84% position:16%
And in many instances
have been linked

00:27:37.889 --> 00:27:39.891
by this administration itself

00:27:39.891 --> 00:27:41.092
to the administration's

00:27:41.092 --> 00:27:43.194 align:start size:84% position:16%
radical climate change
fighting agenda.

00:27:44.362 --> 00:27:47.565
- So when Rand Paul,
for example, said,

00:27:47.565 --> 00:27:50.368 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Hey, we should at least
be able to, you know,

00:27:50.368 --> 00:27:53.371 align:start size:97% position:3%
"know where the money's
going, or audit this thing,

00:27:53.371 --> 00:27:54.873
"or get some receipts",

00:27:54.873 --> 00:27:56.975
and basically the entire
media tried to destroy him

00:27:56.975 --> 00:27:59.110
as if you know, he's
in Putin's pocket.

00:28:00.245 --> 00:28:01.446
I'm guessing you were
pretty sympathetic

00:28:01.446 --> 00:28:02.747
to what he was going through.

00:28:02.747 --> 00:28:04.049
- Absolutely, absolutely.

00:28:04.049 --> 00:28:05.884 align:start size:84% position:16%
Yeah, the same thing
happened to me.

00:28:05.884 --> 00:28:08.820
I mean, the changes
Rand Paul proposed,

00:28:08.820 --> 00:28:12.257 align:start position:0%
let's have an Inspector General
over this, very reasonable.

00:28:12.257 --> 00:28:14.959
In fact, it's hard
for me to imagine

00:28:14.959 --> 00:28:17.062
that that subset of Americans

00:28:17.062 --> 00:28:20.298 align:start size:97% position:3%
who wouldn't want something
like that in place.

00:28:20.298 --> 00:28:21.733
Likewise with my bill,

00:28:23.168 --> 00:28:24.703
there's nothing that should
be terribly objectionable

00:28:24.703 --> 00:28:27.872
about saying we should
offset this spending

00:28:27.872 --> 00:28:30.642
so that we reduce the
inflationary pressures

00:28:30.642 --> 00:28:32.811 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we're exerting
on the economy.

00:28:32.811 --> 00:28:35.213 align:start size:84% position:16%
And as long as
we're passing a bill

00:28:35.213 --> 00:28:37.015
that's supposed to
be about Ukraine,

00:28:37.015 --> 00:28:40.418
it shouldn't be about
further entrenching

00:28:40.418 --> 00:28:41.653
the state department

00:28:41.653 --> 00:28:45.824
and just flooding the
state department itself

00:28:45.824 --> 00:28:47.125
with additional funds.

00:28:48.993 --> 00:28:50.095
- So when you, not to,

00:28:50.095 --> 00:28:51.596
I'm not trying to create a fight

00:28:51.596 --> 00:28:53.832 align:start size:97% position:3%
with you and your counterpart,
your other Senator in Utah,

00:28:53.832 --> 00:28:55.700
but like, did you and
Mitt discuss that?

00:28:55.700 --> 00:28:58.203
And did he say, yeah, I'm
just kind of okay with it,

00:28:58.203 --> 00:28:59.671
or not even about him,

00:28:59.671 --> 00:29:00.872 align:start size:81% position:19%
but just generally
when you spoke

00:29:00.872 --> 00:29:02.474 align:start size:84% position:16%
to some other
Republican Senators

00:29:02.474 --> 00:29:04.142
that just kind of signed
it and were just like,

00:29:04.142 --> 00:29:05.310
ah, we're doing it.

00:29:05.310 --> 00:29:07.045
What do people say?

00:29:07.045 --> 00:29:09.347 align:start size:97% position:3%
- That's the problem that's
one of my biggest frustrations

00:29:09.347 --> 00:29:12.484
with Washington is that
people will sign on to

00:29:12.484 --> 00:29:15.520
what I refer to as the sort
of bumper sticker effect

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:17.088
of a particular bill.

00:29:17.088 --> 00:29:19.491
And for many of my colleagues,

00:29:20.925 --> 00:29:24.028
they looked at this and said,
"Look, Ukraine is important.

00:29:24.028 --> 00:29:26.030
"Ukraine is a sympathetic victim

00:29:26.030 --> 00:29:28.466
"to a vile menace to
human civilization

00:29:28.466 --> 00:29:30.068
"who is Vladimir Putin."

00:29:30.068 --> 00:29:32.971
And Vladimir Putin is
in fact, a vile menace,

00:29:32.971 --> 00:29:35.607 align:start size:88% position:13%
and Ukraine is a
sympathetic victim here.

00:29:36.775 --> 00:29:38.910
But to translate
that into saying,

00:29:38.910 --> 00:29:40.712
because I like X,

00:29:40.712 --> 00:29:45.016
and X would be covered in one
way or another by this bill,

00:29:45.016 --> 00:29:46.918
I must vote for this bill,

00:29:46.918 --> 00:29:50.388 align:start size:88% position:13%
causes us to enact
some very bad policies.

00:29:50.388 --> 00:29:52.791
It causes us to spend
way too much money,

00:29:52.791 --> 00:29:54.759
and it's frankly, one
of the biggest reasons

00:29:54.759 --> 00:29:57.295
why we're $31 trillion in debt.

00:29:57.295 --> 00:29:59.497
These people are
afraid to be seen

00:29:59.497 --> 00:30:02.934 align:start size:88% position:13%
as voting against a
sympathetic beneficiary,

00:30:02.934 --> 00:30:04.602
or a combination thereof.

00:30:04.602 --> 00:30:06.337 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that's how we
get to this problem.

00:30:06.337 --> 00:30:08.873 align:start size:97% position:3%
So it's the basic difference
of opinion that I have

00:30:08.873 --> 00:30:10.308
with some of my colleagues,

00:30:10.308 --> 00:30:11.543
who will look at
something and say,

00:30:11.543 --> 00:30:13.378
"I agree with the
overall objectives,

00:30:13.378 --> 00:30:14.813
"so I'm willing to vote for it,

00:30:14.813 --> 00:30:17.849 align:start size:84% position:16%
"even though this
particular formulation

00:30:17.849 --> 00:30:20.151
"has some serious
problems with it."

00:30:20.151 --> 00:30:21.619
- So since you are a guy

00:30:21.619 --> 00:30:23.621
that cares about
the actual rules

00:30:23.621 --> 00:30:25.156
and the way that we're
supposed to be governed,

00:30:25.156 --> 00:30:27.692
I mean, in effect, it seems
like we're at war right now.

00:30:27.692 --> 00:30:29.127
I mean, we haven't declared war,

00:30:29.127 --> 00:30:31.029
I know the President's
supposed to declare war,

00:30:31.029 --> 00:30:33.064 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it has to get authorized
and everything else,

00:30:33.064 --> 00:30:35.099
but I mean, when you're
pouring tons of money

00:30:35.099 --> 00:30:37.268 align:start size:97% position:3%
into something, when you're
giving people weapons,

00:30:37.268 --> 00:30:40.672
et cetera, et cetera, you
in essence are at war.

00:30:40.672 --> 00:30:43.174 line:0%
I mean, at what point do
we start looking at that,

00:30:43.174 --> 00:30:47.846 align:start position:0% line:0%
and having Congress or the
Senate go, "Boy, are we at war?

00:30:47.846 --> 00:30:49.848
"And how much money do you
have to pour into something

00:30:49.848 --> 00:30:51.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
"before you officially
call it war?"

00:30:51.382 --> 00:30:54.152
If you keep giving a guy
weapons, maybe it is war.

00:30:54.152 --> 00:30:56.454 align:start size:84% position:16%
Or again, is that just
one of those things

00:30:56.454 --> 00:30:58.857 align:start size:84% position:16%
that is just kind
of, it is what it is,

00:30:58.857 --> 00:31:00.391
and the beat goes on.

00:31:00.391 --> 00:31:02.594
- Yeah, I think it's
absolutely essential

00:31:02.594 --> 00:31:05.563 align:start size:84% position:16%
that before we
take any more steps

00:31:05.563 --> 00:31:08.032
in the direction of warfare,

00:31:09.434 --> 00:31:12.403
that we take into account
our role as a Congress

00:31:12.403 --> 00:31:13.905
and declaring war.

00:31:13.905 --> 00:31:17.709
Over many decades, Congress
has gradually relinquished

00:31:17.709 --> 00:31:20.712
its declaration of war powers.

00:31:20.712 --> 00:31:25.316 align:start position:0%
As presidents of both
political parties have asserted

00:31:25.316 --> 00:31:29.721
really broad powers attached
to their inherent authority

00:31:29.721 --> 00:31:34.092
under Article II as commander
in chief of the armed forces.

00:31:34.092 --> 00:31:35.560
This is problematic

00:31:35.560 --> 00:31:38.129 align:start size:97% position:3%
because it was one of the
distinguishing characteristics

00:31:38.129 --> 00:31:39.397
of the Constitution.

00:31:39.397 --> 00:31:40.565
One of the things that
made it very different

00:31:40.565 --> 00:31:41.933
than the English model,

00:31:41.933 --> 00:31:45.003
the system that we had
just broken away from

00:31:45.003 --> 00:31:48.539
allowed the chief executive,
the king, to take us to war.

00:31:48.539 --> 00:31:49.507
And then it was up to parliament

00:31:49.507 --> 00:31:51.342
to figure out how to pay for it.

00:31:51.342 --> 00:31:54.345 align:start size:97% position:3%
Under our system, Federalist
69 makes this very clear

00:31:54.345 --> 00:31:56.781
when Alexander Hamilton explains

00:31:56.781 --> 00:31:58.316
that it was with good reason

00:31:58.316 --> 00:32:01.152
we put the power to declare
war in the first instance

00:32:01.152 --> 00:32:02.687
in the hands of Congress,

00:32:02.687 --> 00:32:05.123 align:start size:97% position:3%
because that's the branch
most accountable to the people

00:32:05.123 --> 00:32:06.891
at the most regular intervals.

00:32:06.891 --> 00:32:09.961
So I think if we're gonna
take any additional steps

00:32:09.961 --> 00:32:11.462
in this area,

00:32:11.462 --> 00:32:13.498
we really ought to have a
conversation about whether,

00:32:13.498 --> 00:32:14.799
in what way, to what extent

00:32:14.799 --> 00:32:17.769
we wanna be involved in
a conflict with Russia.

00:32:17.769 --> 00:32:21.773
Keep in mind, Ukraine spends
less than $5 billion a year

00:32:21.773 --> 00:32:23.274
in defense.

00:32:23.274 --> 00:32:26.844 align:start size:94% position:6%
Russia spends about $65
billion a year in defense.

00:32:26.844 --> 00:32:28.579 align:start size:84% position:16%
If you add up the
previous Ukraine bill,

00:32:28.579 --> 00:32:33.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
14 billion to this
Ukraine bill, 40 billion,

00:32:33.151 --> 00:32:35.720
you're talking about
more than 10 times

00:32:35.720 --> 00:32:39.824
what Ukraine spends on
defense in an entire year

00:32:39.824 --> 00:32:43.561
is what we have devoted in
the name of Ukraine defense.

00:32:43.561 --> 00:32:44.796
And we're knocking on the door

00:32:44.796 --> 00:32:47.532
of what Russia spends
on defense worldwide

00:32:47.532 --> 00:32:49.500
in an entire year.

00:32:49.500 --> 00:32:51.970
- Do you think we're
in a bizarre situation

00:32:51.970 --> 00:32:53.404
as the last superpower

00:32:53.404 --> 00:32:55.807 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we're still expected
to do everything,

00:32:55.807 --> 00:33:00.044
but maybe we kind of just
can't in a certain respect.

00:33:00.044 --> 00:33:01.946
I personally believe that,

00:33:01.946 --> 00:33:03.715 align:start size:84% position:16%
I believe in American
exceptionalism,

00:33:03.715 --> 00:33:05.483 align:start size:94% position:6%
I believe the world is
better when we're leading,

00:33:05.483 --> 00:33:07.952 align:start size:97% position:3%
I believe in peace through
strength, all of those things,

00:33:07.952 --> 00:33:09.354
but that maybe right now,

00:33:09.354 --> 00:33:11.823 align:start size:88% position:13%
especially with the
leadership that we have,

00:33:11.823 --> 00:33:16.828 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we just can't do that
much good at the moment

00:33:17.862 --> 00:33:18.896 align:start size:84% position:16%
in these big
international things.

00:33:18.896 --> 00:33:20.365
- Well yeah, look,

00:33:20.365 --> 00:33:21.933
I think this is where we
get dangerously off course.

00:33:21.933 --> 00:33:24.535 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think it is dangerous
for any country,

00:33:24.535 --> 00:33:27.005
last standing superpower or not,

00:33:27.005 --> 00:33:30.441
to step into the
mindset that says,

00:33:30.441 --> 00:33:32.110 align:start size:84% position:16%
"If there is injustice
in the world,

00:33:32.110 --> 00:33:37.115
"we can be counted on as the
presumptive police force."

00:33:37.849 --> 00:33:38.783
No nation can do that.

00:33:38.783 --> 00:33:40.685
No nation can withstand that.

00:33:40.685 --> 00:33:42.420
Certainly we can't.

00:33:42.420 --> 00:33:46.891 align:start position:0%
Our Constitutional system should
never allow that to happen.

00:33:46.891 --> 00:33:48.659
What really needs to happen

00:33:48.659 --> 00:33:52.063
is that anytime we get
involved militarily,

00:33:52.063 --> 00:33:55.233
we really ought to have
Congress declaring war

00:33:55.233 --> 00:33:57.835 align:start size:94% position:6%
before we take any
action in that direction.

00:33:57.835 --> 00:34:01.172 align:start size:84% position:16%
And always the
inquiry should be on

00:34:01.172 --> 00:34:03.875 align:start size:94% position:6%
not on whether bad people
are doing bad things

00:34:03.875 --> 00:34:05.676
in another part of the world,

00:34:05.676 --> 00:34:08.246 align:start size:97% position:3%
but on how that affects
American national security,

00:34:08.246 --> 00:34:09.847
the safety, security,

00:34:09.847 --> 00:34:12.283 align:start size:88% position:13%
the liberty of the
American people at home.

00:34:13.651 --> 00:34:17.288 align:start position:0%
If we can't connect it to
that, we shouldn't be doing it,

00:34:17.288 --> 00:34:20.925 align:start size:97% position:3%
because there is no end to
the threats that we will face,

00:34:20.925 --> 00:34:23.161 align:start size:81% position:19%
the battles we
will have to fight

00:34:23.161 --> 00:34:25.797 align:start size:84% position:16%
with no real
corresponding ability

00:34:26.731 --> 00:34:28.633
to do all of those things.

00:34:28.633 --> 00:34:30.501
If our only test
is to go out there

00:34:30.501 --> 00:34:32.937
and find bad people
doing bad things,

00:34:32.937 --> 00:34:34.772 align:start size:84% position:16%
we'll be very busy,
so busy indeed,

00:34:34.772 --> 00:34:36.741 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we will leave the
American people vulnerable

00:34:36.741 --> 00:34:38.142
in their own homeland.

00:34:38.142 --> 00:34:40.545
- So speaking of safety
and liberty at home,

00:34:40.545 --> 00:34:44.315
you know, two years or two
and a half years post COVID,

00:34:45.683 --> 00:34:49.887 align:start size:94% position:6%
are you hopeful that
more people have woken up

00:34:49.887 --> 00:34:51.456 align:start size:84% position:16%
to some of the
state's right stuff,

00:34:51.456 --> 00:34:55.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
some of the stuff related
to the courts, and mandates,

00:34:55.026 --> 00:34:57.762 align:start size:84% position:16%
and all of the
overreach that we saw?

00:34:57.762 --> 00:35:00.031
I mean, my sense is
that a lot of people

00:35:00.031 --> 00:35:01.265
really have woken up.

00:35:01.265 --> 00:35:03.468 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't know if they
got scared again,

00:35:03.468 --> 00:35:06.070 align:start size:97% position:3%
if they would just drop
all of their newfound ideas

00:35:06.070 --> 00:35:07.738
very quickly in the
name of security,

00:35:07.738 --> 00:35:10.108 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I'm kind of
hopeful at the moment.

00:35:10.108 --> 00:35:13.845
- Yeah, as my wife,
Sharon would put it,

00:35:13.845 --> 00:35:15.413
all government overreach,

00:35:15.413 --> 00:35:17.782 align:start size:97% position:3%
all socialism and all other
forms of government overreach,

00:35:17.782 --> 00:35:21.819
it always starts out under
the auspices of an emergency.

00:35:21.819 --> 00:35:23.888 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I think that's
the awful truth

00:35:23.888 --> 00:35:25.523
to which the American
people have awakened

00:35:25.523 --> 00:35:26.457
over the last two years.

00:35:26.457 --> 00:35:27.925
They've realized,

00:35:27.925 --> 00:35:29.694 align:start size:97% position:3%
oh my gosh, if they can
call something an emergency,

00:35:29.694 --> 00:35:31.229
they can do anything they want.

00:35:31.229 --> 00:35:33.764
And when government can
do anything it wants,

00:35:33.764 --> 00:35:35.399 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the people
fear the government

00:35:35.399 --> 00:35:40.037 align:start position:0%
rather than the other way
around, horrible things happen.

00:35:40.037 --> 00:35:42.140
So yeah, I think
that has created

00:35:42.140 --> 00:35:45.209 align:start size:97% position:3%
a number of accidental
Conservatives and Libertarians

00:35:45.209 --> 00:35:46.577
in this country,

00:35:46.577 --> 00:35:48.546 align:start size:97% position:3%
is they've seen that
horrible abuses of government

00:35:48.546 --> 00:35:53.084
as it goes unquestioned,
all onto the auspices of,

00:35:53.084 --> 00:35:55.887
well, this is necessary
because of an emergency.

00:35:55.887 --> 00:35:58.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
- What do we do about
organizations like the CDC

00:35:58.156 --> 00:35:59.423
and the FDA?

00:35:59.423 --> 00:36:01.159
I mean, they're not
legislative bodies,

00:36:01.159 --> 00:36:03.628
but in essence it seems
like they legislate.

00:36:03.628 --> 00:36:05.229
And then they, it seems like,

00:36:05.229 --> 00:36:07.532
well, you just turn
on any corporate news

00:36:07.532 --> 00:36:10.735
and they're all sponsored by
Pfizer, sponsored by Moderna

00:36:10.735 --> 00:36:14.438
as they're doing stories
on Pfizer, on Moderna.

00:36:14.438 --> 00:36:16.307
What do we do about that mess?

00:36:16.307 --> 00:36:17.842
- Yeah, it's a great point.

00:36:17.842 --> 00:36:20.278
And by the way, I talk
about this a fair amount

00:36:20.278 --> 00:36:21.546
in Saving Nine.

00:36:21.546 --> 00:36:22.813
That's why I like
to say Saving Nine

00:36:22.813 --> 00:36:23.981
is not just about
the Supreme Court,

00:36:23.981 --> 00:36:25.616 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's not just about
court packing.

00:36:25.616 --> 00:36:27.952
It explains all of the maladies

00:36:27.952 --> 00:36:32.023
that flow inevitably
from court packing,

00:36:32.023 --> 00:36:35.893
and flowed from FDR's
court packing threat

00:36:35.893 --> 00:36:37.328
back in the 1930s.

00:36:37.328 --> 00:36:39.130 align:start size:88% position:13%
As a result of that,
we've got too much power

00:36:39.130 --> 00:36:41.999
that's been moved from
the people to Washington,

00:36:41.999 --> 00:36:43.467
then within Washington,

00:36:43.467 --> 00:36:46.904 align:start size:97% position:3%
too much power that has been
voluntarily relinquished

00:36:46.904 --> 00:36:48.839 align:start size:81% position:19%
by the people's
elected lawmakers,

00:36:48.839 --> 00:36:52.009
and handed out to unelected,
unaccountable bureaucrats,

00:36:52.009 --> 00:36:55.046
people at the CDC, and the FDA,

00:36:55.046 --> 00:36:57.114
or the two that you mentioned.

00:36:57.114 --> 00:37:00.551 align:start size:97% position:3%
You noticed of late it's
been getting worse and worse,

00:37:00.551 --> 00:37:02.720 align:start size:81% position:19%
as the Centers for
Disease Control

00:37:02.720 --> 00:37:07.091 align:start size:97% position:3%
is a whole lot more about
the control than the disease.

00:37:07.091 --> 00:37:09.227
And the FDA, rather
than just looking out

00:37:09.227 --> 00:37:13.064
for protecting the American
people from unsafe products,

00:37:13.064 --> 00:37:14.999
is all about controlling them,

00:37:14.999 --> 00:37:17.134
controlling even the information

00:37:17.134 --> 00:37:19.337
to which they may gain access.

00:37:19.337 --> 00:37:21.439 align:start size:88% position:13%
This is the predictable
foreseeable,

00:37:21.439 --> 00:37:23.274
and I believe avoidable result

00:37:23.274 --> 00:37:26.244
of Congress outsourcing
the task of legislation.

00:37:26.244 --> 00:37:29.480
We have for decades
had a Congress that's
in effect saying,

00:37:29.480 --> 00:37:32.416 align:start size:97% position:3%
we hereby declare that we
shall have good law in area X,

00:37:32.416 --> 00:37:37.221
and we hereby delegate to
Commission or Department Y,

00:37:37.221 --> 00:37:40.558 align:start size:84% position:16%
the power to make
good law in that area.

00:37:40.558 --> 00:37:43.027
And then they get to make
the law and enforce it.

00:37:43.027 --> 00:37:45.296
That's a recipe for tyranny.

00:37:46.697 --> 00:37:49.800
- So going on the assumption
that there will be a COVID two,

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:51.736
or another version
of something this,

00:37:51.736 --> 00:37:53.004
or as your wife would say,

00:37:53.004 --> 00:37:55.139
something else that will
scare us in the future,

00:37:55.139 --> 00:37:58.175
what actual authority
does the CDC have?

00:37:58.175 --> 00:37:59.377
I think that was
one of the things

00:37:59.377 --> 00:38:00.878
that really confused people,

00:38:00.878 --> 00:38:03.447
that every day, someone
from the CDC will en ski,

00:38:03.447 --> 00:38:05.016 align:start size:84% position:16%
or one of these people
would get up there,

00:38:05.016 --> 00:38:07.952
they would say something and
then people automatically felt,

00:38:07.952 --> 00:38:10.755
oh, that's what they
said, we have to do it,

00:38:10.755 --> 00:38:12.957
but that's not exactly how
this thing works, right?

00:38:12.957 --> 00:38:14.325
- It's not exactly how it works,

00:38:14.325 --> 00:38:18.362 align:start size:88% position:13%
but there's enough
legislation on the books

00:38:18.362 --> 00:38:22.533
that gives these government
agencies, including the CDC,

00:38:22.533 --> 00:38:27.305
at least a colorable
claim to the authority

00:38:27.305 --> 00:38:30.775
to dictate how much
of our economy runs,

00:38:30.775 --> 00:38:35.646
that they can get by
the initial smell test.

00:38:35.646 --> 00:38:37.348 align:start size:88% position:13%
And see, not everything
gets litigated.

00:38:37.348 --> 00:38:38.916
That's another point
I make in Saving Nine,

00:38:38.916 --> 00:38:40.418
not every dispute,

00:38:40.418 --> 00:38:43.187 align:start size:88% position:13%
not every question
about constitutionality

00:38:43.187 --> 00:38:44.722
goes to the Supreme Court.

00:38:44.722 --> 00:38:46.590
Most cases don't
even get litigated,

00:38:46.590 --> 00:38:49.794
the very small percentage
of those that get litigated

00:38:49.794 --> 00:38:51.896
make their way all the
way to the Supreme Court.

00:38:51.896 --> 00:38:54.965 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it can take months
or sometimes years

00:38:54.965 --> 00:38:57.702
to get to the bottom of
some of these problems.

00:38:57.702 --> 00:39:00.538 align:start position:0%
In the meantime, a lot of these
disputes have become moot,

00:39:00.538 --> 00:39:03.774
and the courts then start to
lack jurisdiction over them.

00:39:03.774 --> 00:39:07.445
That's why we need
genuine deep reform,

00:39:07.445 --> 00:39:10.147
where we withdraw the authority,

00:39:10.147 --> 00:39:12.750 align:start size:88% position:13%
the delegated authority
to make new law.

00:39:12.750 --> 00:39:14.585
It's one of the reasons
why I believe so strongly

00:39:14.585 --> 00:39:16.954 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the need to pass
things like the REINS Act.

00:39:16.954 --> 00:39:18.989
Look, if I were in
charge of Congress,

00:39:18.989 --> 00:39:23.994
and I had just one
legislative measure

00:39:25.196 --> 00:39:27.064
that I could pick and
guarantee passage of,

00:39:27.064 --> 00:39:28.432
it would be the REINS Act.

00:39:28.432 --> 00:39:30.501
The acronym is R-E-I-N-S,

00:39:30.501 --> 00:39:32.236 align:start size:84% position:16%
stands for Regulations
from the Executive

00:39:32.236 --> 00:39:33.738
In Need of Scrutiny.

00:39:33.738 --> 00:39:35.005
To make a long story short,

00:39:35.005 --> 00:39:37.908
it says that we're
not going to allow

00:39:37.908 --> 00:39:39.710 align:start size:88% position:13%
unelected unaccountable
bureaucrats

00:39:39.710 --> 00:39:41.078
to make our laws anymore.

00:39:41.078 --> 00:39:43.013
That will be done
only by Congress.

00:39:43.013 --> 00:39:46.851
And when an executive branch
agency uses delegate authority

00:39:46.851 --> 00:39:49.553
to create what is,
in effect, a new law

00:39:49.553 --> 00:39:51.522
that will not be self-executing

00:39:51.522 --> 00:39:53.057
after the passage
of the REINS Act,

00:39:53.057 --> 00:39:55.559 align:start size:97% position:3%
it will require an
affirmative vote by Congress

00:39:55.559 --> 00:39:56.961
to pass it into law.

00:39:56.961 --> 00:39:58.496
You see, people can fire

00:39:58.496 --> 00:40:01.031 align:start size:88% position:13%
their elected
lawmakers periodically.

00:40:01.031 --> 00:40:03.000 align:start size:84% position:16%
You can't fire a
government bureaucrat,

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:04.935 align:start size:81% position:19%
that's why we need
the REINS Act.

00:40:04.935 --> 00:40:06.837 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, that's also
sort of related

00:40:06.837 --> 00:40:08.539
to something else
you said before

00:40:08.539 --> 00:40:10.040 align:start size:84% position:16%
about how our system
is different than
the British system,

00:40:10.040 --> 00:40:14.011
and yet in some ways we kind
of yearn for more control,

00:40:14.011 --> 00:40:16.614 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's a weird paradox
that I think humans have.

00:40:16.614 --> 00:40:17.982
I mean, you hear it all the time

00:40:17.982 --> 00:40:19.216
during presidential elections,

00:40:19.216 --> 00:40:20.651
what I'm gonna do the
first day in office.

00:40:20.651 --> 00:40:23.387 align:start position:0%
And it's like, well wait a
minute, you can't do that much.

00:40:23.387 --> 00:40:25.456
You're not supposed to
be able to do that much.

00:40:25.456 --> 00:40:27.825 align:start size:97% position:3%
But then they do everything
by executive action.

00:40:27.825 --> 00:40:30.094 align:start position:0%
I mean, I assume that's another
one that you've feel like

00:40:30.094 --> 00:40:32.263
has really just we've
let it out of the bag,

00:40:32.263 --> 00:40:33.764
and now it's out
of control, right?

00:40:33.764 --> 00:40:36.133 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yes, it's not just that
we've let it out of the bag,

00:40:36.133 --> 00:40:39.270
Congress has now blessed it,
Congress has ordained this

00:40:39.270 --> 00:40:41.105
as a legitimate law
making exercise.

00:40:41.105 --> 00:40:44.375
You know, I keep two stacks
of documents in my office

00:40:44.375 --> 00:40:45.910
in the Senate.

00:40:45.910 --> 00:40:48.879
Well, one stack is short,
it's a few inches tall,

00:40:48.879 --> 00:40:50.147
it's usually a few hundred,

00:40:50.147 --> 00:40:52.216 align:start size:84% position:16%
sometimes a few
thousand pages long.

00:40:52.216 --> 00:40:56.187 align:start position:0%
It's the laws passed by Congress
during the previous year.

00:40:56.187 --> 00:41:01.091 align:start size:88% position:13%
The other stack,
currently 13 feet tall,

00:41:02.526 --> 00:41:06.197
it's often approaching, if
not exceeding 100,000 pages,

00:41:07.364 --> 00:41:08.866 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it's last year's
federal register.

00:41:08.866 --> 00:41:11.936
That's the accumulative annual
index of federal regulations

00:41:11.936 --> 00:41:16.440
as they're proposed, measured
by height, weight, volume,

00:41:16.440 --> 00:41:19.944
word count, economic
impact, you name it.

00:41:19.944 --> 00:41:23.147 align:start size:84% position:16%
The executive
branch bureaucracies

00:41:23.147 --> 00:41:25.249
create more law every year

00:41:25.249 --> 00:41:27.151
than the people's
elected lawmakers.

00:41:27.151 --> 00:41:30.554 align:start position:0%
But my copy of the Constitution
in Article I Section One

00:41:30.554 --> 00:41:33.524 align:start size:88% position:13%
and Article I Section
Seven make really clear,

00:41:33.524 --> 00:41:35.626
you're not supposed to be
able to make a federal law

00:41:35.626 --> 00:41:37.194
without Congress.

00:41:37.194 --> 00:41:39.330 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So how do we clear
out some of that?

00:41:39.330 --> 00:41:40.731
How do we clear out
some of that brush?

00:41:40.731 --> 00:41:42.633 align:start size:88% position:13%
How do we get rid of
that bureaucratic state,

00:41:42.633 --> 00:41:43.968
or the deep state or whatever,

00:41:43.968 --> 00:41:45.269
or maybe not even get rid of it

00:41:45.269 --> 00:41:47.805
because some version of
something for stability

00:41:47.805 --> 00:41:49.073
has to exist, but how,

00:41:49.073 --> 00:41:50.708
it seems like it's just
getting bigger, right?

00:41:50.708 --> 00:41:52.510 align:start size:84% position:16%
We talked about
draining the swamp,

00:41:52.510 --> 00:41:54.745 align:start size:94% position:6%
maybe there was some
degree of it under Trump,

00:41:54.745 --> 00:41:56.547 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it certainly
seems like it's back

00:41:56.547 --> 00:41:58.048
and bigger than ever right now.

00:41:58.048 --> 00:41:59.950 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, is there, do you have
any levers at this point,

00:41:59.950 --> 00:42:01.185 align:start size:84% position:16%
does anyone that
cares about this stuff

00:42:01.185 --> 00:42:03.120
have any levers to do anything?

00:42:03.120 --> 00:42:05.055 line:0%
- Well, they do only insofar

00:42:05.055 --> 00:42:06.690 line:0%
as the American
people demand it.

00:42:06.690 --> 00:42:08.158 line:0%
And once they demand it,

00:42:08.158 --> 00:42:10.661 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
it will become inevitable
that it will happen.

00:42:10.661 --> 00:42:13.864
That's why I think the best
single thing we could do,

00:42:13.864 --> 00:42:16.800 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think the agenda
that Republicans
ought to be running on

00:42:16.800 --> 00:42:18.836
is passage of the REINS Act,

00:42:18.836 --> 00:42:20.871
because that would
force a rebalancing

00:42:20.871 --> 00:42:22.339
of this balance of power

00:42:22.339 --> 00:42:25.809 align:start size:94% position:6%
between the executive
and legislative branches.

00:42:25.809 --> 00:42:27.311
Too much of the power
has been given over

00:42:27.311 --> 00:42:28.679
to the executive branch,

00:42:28.679 --> 00:42:31.048 align:start size:97% position:3%
that's why we become
vulnerable to all this stuff.

00:42:31.048 --> 00:42:33.384
I mean, let's take, for example,

00:42:33.384 --> 00:42:36.120 align:start size:88% position:13%
one of the worst abuses
I have ever seen,

00:42:36.120 --> 00:42:38.155 align:start size:88% position:13%
one of the worst abuses
of executive power

00:42:38.155 --> 00:42:41.525
that's ever existed in the
history of our Republic,

00:42:41.525 --> 00:42:45.696
and that was President
Biden's vaccine mandates.

00:42:45.696 --> 00:42:47.531 align:start size:84% position:16%
Perhaps the most
egregious among them

00:42:47.531 --> 00:42:49.333 align:start size:94% position:6%
was the one that
affected the most people,

00:42:49.333 --> 00:42:52.269
a hundred million people or so,

00:42:52.269 --> 00:42:55.673
the so-called OSHA
vaccine mandate,

00:42:55.673 --> 00:42:57.107 align:start size:84% position:16%
where they
arbitrarily determined

00:42:57.107 --> 00:43:00.210
that any employer with
more than 99 workers

00:43:00.210 --> 00:43:03.213 align:start size:94% position:6%
had to require vaccination
of all their employees.

00:43:03.213 --> 00:43:04.782
And that any that didn't would--

00:43:04.782 --> 00:43:06.417
- Wait Senator, are you
telling me that's not,

00:43:06.417 --> 00:43:07.952
are you telling me
that's not science?

00:43:07.952 --> 00:43:11.522
95 and then to 99, I'd have
to look at the science here.

00:43:11.522 --> 00:43:13.724 align:start size:97% position:3%
Are you telling me there was
something not scientific?

00:43:13.724 --> 00:43:15.259
- There was nothing
scientific about it,

00:43:15.259 --> 00:43:17.127
the virus isn't that smart.

00:43:17.127 --> 00:43:18.729
The virus doesn't
have the ability

00:43:18.729 --> 00:43:20.598
to differentiate
between workplaces

00:43:20.598 --> 00:43:24.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
with more than 99 workers
and those without.

00:43:24.134 --> 00:43:27.004
And the Biden administration
wasn't smart enough to realize

00:43:27.004 --> 00:43:29.707 align:start position:0%
it's not within their
Constitutional power to do that,

00:43:29.707 --> 00:43:34.712 align:start position:0%
but they led claim based on
this statute that created OSHA,

00:43:36.046 --> 00:43:38.782 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, or the executive
branch bureaucracy

00:43:38.782 --> 00:43:41.452 align:start size:84% position:16%
in charge of
occupational safety.

00:43:41.452 --> 00:43:46.323 align:start size:94% position:6%
And OSHA promulgated
using this broad statute,

00:43:46.323 --> 00:43:50.094
giving OSHA the power to
make workplaces safer,

00:43:50.094 --> 00:43:52.696 align:start size:88% position:13%
this emergency
temporary order saying,

00:43:52.696 --> 00:43:56.300 align:start size:94% position:6%
we find this is necessary,
therefore make it so.

00:43:56.300 --> 00:43:59.169
That's exactly the kind of
thing that does violence

00:43:59.169 --> 00:44:00.804
to our Constitutional
system of government

00:44:00.804 --> 00:44:03.540
and separates the people from
their own form of government.

00:44:03.540 --> 00:44:05.576 align:start size:84% position:16%
Things like that
have to be repealed,

00:44:05.576 --> 00:44:09.179 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the best way to
start that ball rolling

00:44:09.179 --> 00:44:11.548
is to enact the REINS Act,

00:44:11.548 --> 00:44:12.983
which again would make it

00:44:12.983 --> 00:44:17.021 align:start size:97% position:3%
so that any new substantive
change to federal law

00:44:17.021 --> 00:44:20.090
would have to be affirmatively
enacted into law by Congress.

00:44:20.090 --> 00:44:21.892
- Are you ever amazed
that nobody gets fired

00:44:21.892 --> 00:44:23.360
from any of these organizations?

00:44:23.360 --> 00:44:25.796
I mean, two years after
COVID, as far as I know,

00:44:25.796 --> 00:44:27.998
no one really at
the CDC got fired,

00:44:27.998 --> 00:44:31.502
nobody at the FDA got fired.

00:44:31.502 --> 00:44:33.904
You know, nobody involved
at the governmental level

00:44:33.904 --> 00:44:36.940
who were pushing mandates
that either were illegal,

00:44:36.940 --> 00:44:38.042
you know, potentially illegal,

00:44:38.042 --> 00:44:39.943
or at the very least immoral,

00:44:39.943 --> 00:44:41.178
the series of other things,

00:44:41.178 --> 00:44:42.946
you know, they forced
doctors out of work,

00:44:42.946 --> 00:44:44.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then, you know, first
they were essential,

00:44:44.448 --> 00:44:45.616
then they were out of work,

00:44:45.616 --> 00:44:47.651 align:start size:84% position:16%
all of the nonsense
that they did,

00:44:47.651 --> 00:44:49.586 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I don't know that
anyone got fired.

00:44:49.586 --> 00:44:50.921 align:start size:81% position:19%
I don't know of
any other business

00:44:50.921 --> 00:44:52.523
that could operate this way.

00:44:52.523 --> 00:44:54.491 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Right, but why
would they get fired?

00:44:54.491 --> 00:44:57.261
I mean, this is a
feature, not a bug.

00:44:57.261 --> 00:44:59.263
This is exactly what
progressives want.

00:44:59.263 --> 00:45:01.298
Progressives want to
empower and encourage

00:45:01.298 --> 00:45:02.566
that kind of thinking.

00:45:02.566 --> 00:45:04.601
So of course they
didn't get fired.

00:45:04.601 --> 00:45:06.370
Look, they won't get fired,

00:45:06.370 --> 00:45:08.472 align:start size:97% position:3%
they won't lose power
until the people demand it,

00:45:08.472 --> 00:45:11.375
and Congress acting on
the demands of the people

00:45:11.375 --> 00:45:12.943
relieves them of duty.

00:45:12.943 --> 00:45:16.547
- When Judge, oh gosh,
I'm thinking, Mizelle,

00:45:16.547 --> 00:45:17.715
I almost forgot her
name for a second,

00:45:17.715 --> 00:45:19.283 align:start size:84% position:16%
and everyone should
know her name.

00:45:19.283 --> 00:45:24.288 align:start position:0%
The 35 year old Trump-appointed
federal judge in Tampa,

00:45:24.288 --> 00:45:28.192
when she reversed the
airline mask mandate,

00:45:28.192 --> 00:45:30.227
I had just started my book tour.

00:45:30.227 --> 00:45:31.795 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was going on like
two days later,

00:45:31.795 --> 00:45:33.297
so I was gonna be
on a lot of planes.

00:45:33.297 --> 00:45:35.199
I was as thrilled as anybody.

00:45:35.199 --> 00:45:38.402
And what was amazing to
me was that the next day,

00:45:38.402 --> 00:45:41.105
virtually I would say 90%
of people if not more,

00:45:41.105 --> 00:45:43.040 align:start size:81% position:19%
ripped their masks
off, you know,

00:45:43.040 --> 00:45:45.943 align:start size:84% position:16%
a week before Fauci
had just said,

00:45:45.943 --> 00:45:48.479 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Oh, we're reviewing it again,
but we're gonna extend it."

00:45:48.479 --> 00:45:51.749
He had just extended the
airline mask mandate thing.

00:45:53.150 --> 00:45:54.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
But meanwhile, there's
been no outbreak of COVID,

00:45:54.852 --> 00:45:57.855 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, no new sudden
surge or anything like that.

00:45:57.855 --> 00:46:00.023 align:start size:84% position:16%
And all of the people,
we ripped it off,

00:46:00.023 --> 00:46:01.291
and everyone was smiling again,

00:46:01.291 --> 00:46:03.060 align:start size:84% position:16%
and talking on planes
and at airports,

00:46:03.060 --> 00:46:05.062
restaurants are packed again.

00:46:05.062 --> 00:46:07.564 align:start size:97% position:3%
This is the type of thing that
I think makes people crazy.

00:46:07.564 --> 00:46:11.602
It's like, where was this one
brave woman two years ago?

00:46:11.602 --> 00:46:13.537 align:start size:84% position:16%
Where, did you talk
to any judges and say,

00:46:13.537 --> 00:46:15.939
"Hey, somebody could do
something about this."

00:46:15.939 --> 00:46:18.075
You know, I know you can't
exert influence over them,

00:46:18.075 --> 00:46:21.712
but did you talk to anybody
over a scotch somewhere in DC

00:46:21.712 --> 00:46:24.214 align:start size:88% position:13%
and say, "Hey, you
could flip this thing".

00:46:24.214 --> 00:46:28.018 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, I talked at great
length to the media,

00:46:28.018 --> 00:46:29.453
to my colleagues,

00:46:29.453 --> 00:46:31.188 align:start size:97% position:3%
to executive branch personnel
over the last two years

00:46:31.188 --> 00:46:32.723 align:start size:81% position:19%
about the fact
that it was wrong,

00:46:32.723 --> 00:46:35.459
about the fact that it was at
best a stretch of federal law,

00:46:35.459 --> 00:46:37.227
and acting on a federal law

00:46:37.227 --> 00:46:39.963
that should never have
existed to begin with.

00:46:39.963 --> 00:46:42.099 align:start size:94% position:6%
But that's the thing
about our judicial system.

00:46:42.099 --> 00:46:44.168
They can't just go out and act,

00:46:44.168 --> 00:46:46.136 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's one of the
things that I explain

00:46:46.136 --> 00:46:47.771
in chapter one of Saving Nine.

00:46:47.771 --> 00:46:50.073 align:start size:84% position:16%
The courts aren't just
a roving commission

00:46:50.073 --> 00:46:52.109 align:start size:84% position:16%
to smack down things
they don't like.

00:46:52.109 --> 00:46:54.912
They've got to wait until
an actual ripe controversy

00:46:54.912 --> 00:46:56.213
is brought before them,

00:46:56.213 --> 00:46:58.182
a dispute over the
meaning of the law.

00:46:58.182 --> 00:47:00.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it can take a
significant amount of time

00:47:00.517 --> 00:47:02.019
to find the right plaintiff,

00:47:02.019 --> 00:47:04.321
for that plaintiff to come
forward and present a case,

00:47:04.321 --> 00:47:06.824 align:start size:88% position:13%
and for the appropriate
motions to be made.

00:47:06.824 --> 00:47:09.893 align:start size:94% position:6%
And Judge Mizelle happened
to be the first one

00:47:09.893 --> 00:47:11.528
to act on one of those,
and I applaud her for it,

00:47:11.528 --> 00:47:12.796
it was a correct ruling.

00:47:13.764 --> 00:47:15.499
- The book is Saving Nine,

00:47:15.499 --> 00:47:17.634
Senator Mike Lee, as I
said at the beginning,

00:47:17.634 --> 00:47:19.803
you're on the short list
of sane people over there,

00:47:19.803 --> 00:47:22.706
so I wish you luck.

00:47:22.706 --> 00:47:24.141
- Thank you.

00:47:24.141 --> 00:47:25.375
- My pleasure.

